  \widehat{\mathrm{SE}}^2&=\frac{\widehat\Var(Y)}{M}+\Bigl(\frac1n-\frac1M\Bigr)\widehat\Var(h),
\end{align}
with $\widehat\Var(Y)$ the labeled sample variance and $\widehat\Var(h)$ the variance of the pooled out-of-fold residuals. The interval is $\hat\theta\pm z_{1-\alpha/2}\widehat{\mathrm{SE}}$ unless another construction is named.

\paragraph{Validity needs stability, not accuracy.}
Because the propensity is known, \eqref{eq:Vg} holds for the limit of $\hat g$ whatever that limit is: if $\hat g$ converges in $L^2$ to some fixed $g_{\lim}$ (cross-fit stability), with i.i.d.\ records, MCAR labels and finite second moments, the interval is asymptotically valid. A poor calibrator therefore costs width ($V(g_{\lim})>\Vstar$), not coverage. This is the property that makes the headroom comparison fair to PPI++: both are valid, and what differs is width. In finite samples the learning noise in $\hat g$ is not covered by this argument, which is why coverage is measured in every cell (\cref{sec:experiments}). We also run the package's PPI++ (\texttt{ppi-python}) on $\hat g(f)$ in place of $f$, which should match the instrument's width up to the estimate of~$\lambda$.

\paragraph{Interval construction as a second factor.}
Width is the standard error times a critical value. For bounded or binary $Y$ the Wald construction is not the only choice (Wilson-type, bootstrap-$t$, $t$ quantiles around the control-variate estimate), and these may be shorter and better calibrated at small $n$ or extreme proportions for every method including PPI++. We report the headroom as two factors: the standard-error factor (from $g$) and the construction factor.
